to how do we find the next new thing out there?

511
00:27:51,000 --> 00:27:53,000
We'll get into that later.

512
00:27:53,000 --> 00:27:57,000
But here's the data on this,

513
00:27:57,000 --> 00:28:01,000
where you see that basically these are all the major improvement innovations.

514
00:28:01,000 --> 00:28:05,000
All the first ones were done by users and the major improvements, 80% of them.

515
00:28:05,000 --> 00:28:07,000
Now, to say done by users,

516
00:28:07,000 --> 00:28:10,000
we don't mean that the user just had the idea.

517
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:13,000
We mean that the user built it and used it.

518
00:28:13,000 --> 00:28:15,000
Actually built it.

519
00:28:15,000 --> 00:28:17,000
Now, what I'm going to do to give you a feeling for this

520
00:28:17,000 --> 00:28:20,000
is I'm going to show you some examples.

521
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:23,000
And then you're going to, as I say, have your own.

522
00:28:23,000 --> 00:28:25,000
OK?

523
00:28:25,000 --> 00:28:28,000
So, here we go.

524
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:31,000
Here's an automated radioimmunoassay system.

525
00:28:31,000 --> 00:28:34,000
This was actually done.

526
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
It's an example of a scientific instrument system.

527
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
It was the first one.

528
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:42,000
It was done in the University of Virginia

529
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:46,000
by people who had to do a heart study

530
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:50,000
and they had to do 20,000 analyses.

531
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:55,000
Now, before this, these analyses were done by hand.

532
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:58,000
And you can get a graduate student to do a lot of stuff.

533
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:03,000
But you can't get even a graduate student to do it 20,000 times.

534
00:29:03,000 --> 00:29:05,000
So, what do you do?

535
00:29:05,000 --> 00:29:09,000
You start to work and make something that will do it for you.

536
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:12,000
Necessity is the mother of invention, right?

537
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:15,000
So, what they did, though, is they didn't start from scratch

538
00:29:15,000 --> 00:29:19,000
and nothing, they took pieces of things that were around.

539
00:29:19,000 --> 00:29:21,000
Right? They took a little sampling thing here.

540
00:29:21,000 --> 00:29:23,000
They took a pump that was commercial.

541
00:29:23,000 --> 00:29:25,000
They took valves and so on.

542
00:29:25,000 --> 00:29:27,000
They took a computer that was commercial.

543
00:29:27,000 --> 00:29:29,000
And they built the pieces and put it together

544
00:29:29,000 --> 00:29:31,000
to do what they needed to do.

545
00:29:31,000 --> 00:29:36,000
Now, we begin to understand why companies might think that they do it.

546
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:39,000
Because this thing, this user prototype,

547
00:29:39,000 --> 00:29:42,000
is not ready to have Hewlett Packard slapped on the corner.

548
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
Can't ship it like this.

549
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:47,000
Got to do a lot. Looks better later.

550
00:29:47,000 --> 00:29:51,000
But the operating principles are there.

551
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:55,000
This is the way that the commercial one was built.

552
00:29:55,000 --> 00:29:59,000
Now, once we began to understand this in scientific instruments,

553
00:29:59,000 --> 00:30:01,000
we began to see these things all over the place.

554
00:30:01,000 --> 00:30:05,000
Anybody know what that is?

555
00:30:05,000 --> 00:30:08,000
Any clue?

556
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:12,000
Do you remember we used to have a Mount St. Helens?

557
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:14,000
And then it turned to dust one day.

558
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,000
Remember it blew up?

559
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:18,000
This is two days later.

560
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:23,000
And basically what you see is user innovation.

561
00:30:23,000 --> 00:30:25,000
These are police cars.

562
00:30:25,000 --> 00:30:30,000
And what we have here is the problem was that the cars were stopping

563
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:32,000
because there was so much dust in the air.

564
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:35,000
There was so much dust in the air that the air filter of the car

565
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:38,000
that was normally there would just immediately have blocked

566
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,000
and the cars wouldn't run.

567
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:44,000
So this is two days later and the solution is not fully standardized,

568
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
do you understand?

569
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:51,000
But what's going on here is that they're taking what's around to solve their problem.

570
00:30:51,000 --> 00:30:54,000
So this is the kind of a hose that we use in the United States

571
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:57,000
to attach our dryer to the wall, our clothes dryer.

572
00:30:57,000 --> 00:30:59,000
Flexible plastic hose.

573
00:30:59,000 --> 00:31:03,000
This is duct tape, you know, the universal anti-solvent.

574
00:31:03,000 --> 00:31:06,000
And you can see here what happens is one end goes to the engine

575
00:31:06,000 --> 00:31:09,000
and the other end goes to the big filter system.

576
00:31:09,000 --> 00:31:14,000
So here appears to go to the prisoner's lungs as a pre-cleaning device.

577
00:31:14,000 --> 00:31:17,000
But actually what's going on there is I called them up and asked.

578
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:19,000
I was trying to get my data firsthand.

579
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:21,000
They said, no, no, no, we would never do that.

580
00:31:21,000 --> 00:31:22,000
We're not that dumb.

581
00:31:22,000 --> 00:31:24,000
So what we did was we ran the hose and it goes into the trunk

582
00:31:24,000 --> 00:31:26,000
through the back thing, whatever.

583
00:31:26,000 --> 00:31:28,000
So they have a big filter.

584
00:31:28,000 --> 00:31:33,000
Now why do you suppose that the manufacturer didn't do that?

585
00:31:33,000 --> 00:31:37,000
Why didn't the user write a letter to the purulator company

586
00:31:37,000 --> 00:31:41,000
saying we've got trouble out here, want to do something?

587
00:31:41,000 --> 00:31:45,000
Why do you think? Anybody got any reasons?

588
00:31:45,000 --> 00:31:47,000
It would take forever.

589
00:31:47,000 --> 00:31:49,000
They needed it now.

590
00:31:49,000 --> 00:31:51,000
Yeah. Why would it take forever?

591
00:31:51,000 --> 00:31:53,000
Because the company would have to discuss it,

592
00:31:53,000 --> 00:31:56,000
design it, discuss it again, review it,

593
00:31:56,000 --> 00:32:01,000
put together a prototype, test it, market test it,

594
00:32:01,000 --> 00:32:04,000
advertise it, sell it.

595
00:32:04,000 --> 00:32:10,000
So something about the fact that you couldn't get it,

596
00:32:10,000 --> 00:32:13,000
you had to do it yourself.

597
00:32:13,000 --> 00:32:15,000
So they did.

598
00:32:15,000 --> 00:32:17,000
So now we begin to get an inkling

599
00:32:17,000 --> 00:32:21,000
about why users might do these things for themselves.

600
00:32:21,000 --> 00:32:23,000
What you'll find is not all users,

601
00:32:23,000 --> 00:32:27,000
but users in a jam, necessity is the mother of invention,

602
00:32:27,000 --> 00:32:33,000
are often the beginning edges of major innovations.

603
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:38,000
Anybody know what those circles are?

604
00:32:38,000 --> 00:32:41,000
What? Who's got an idea?

605
00:32:41,000 --> 00:32:44,000
Farms, yeah. You know what the circles are?

606
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:47,000
Yeah. Yeah.

607
00:32:47,000 --> 00:32:52,000
It's not a crop circle in the traditional sense.

608
00:32:52,000 --> 00:32:56,000
Here it is in Saudi Arabia for greater contrast.

609
00:32:56,000 --> 00:33:02,000
Basically it was a major innovation in agriculture.

610
00:33:02,000 --> 00:33:07,000
And the great insight was that, gee,

611
00:33:07,000 --> 00:33:13,000
we have an aquifer in lots of areas that's not that deep.

612
00:33:13,000 --> 00:33:17,000
You know, it's underlying our agricultural land.

613
00:33:17,000 --> 00:33:20,000
And what they used to do was they used to dig a well,

614
00:33:20,000 --> 00:33:23,000
and then they would run pipes all over the place.

615
00:33:23,000 --> 00:33:25,000
And then they would have to pick all the pipes up

616
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:27,000
and move them over and pump again and so on and so forth.

617
00:33:27,000 --> 00:33:29,000
Big deal, big mess.

618
00:33:29,000 --> 00:33:33,000
Basically they were using the well as a scarce resource.

619
00:33:33,000 --> 00:33:35,000
But what these guys figured out was, you know,

620
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:38,000
we can go 20 feet down into the ground and we've got a well,

621
00:33:38,000 --> 00:33:41,000
so why don't we sort of make giant sprinkler systems

622
00:33:41,000 --> 00:33:43,000
with wells all over the place?

623
00:33:43,000 --> 00:33:46,000
And so that was a major sort of conceptual breakthrough,

624
00:33:46,000 --> 00:33:47,000
and it was done by a farmer.

625
00:33:47,000 --> 00:33:49,000
And here's the first one.

626
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:53,000
Isn't that cool?

627
00:33:53,000 --> 00:33:56,000
Now you can see by looking at that,

628
00:33:56,000 --> 00:33:59,000
that again, what the farmer has done

629
00:33:59,000 --> 00:34:03,000
is taken stuff that was around, right?

630
00:34:03,000 --> 00:34:06,000
That's the old agricultural pipe.

631
00:34:06,000 --> 00:34:08,000
And the reason this is a favorite of mine

632
00:34:08,000 --> 00:34:10,000
is in one of the things I did in my misspent youth

633
00:34:10,000 --> 00:34:12,000
is I was actually on a ranch.

634
00:34:12,000 --> 00:34:16,000
And we did this process.

635
00:34:16,000 --> 00:34:18,000
You know, you'd actually, you'd have a tractor

636
00:34:18,000 --> 00:34:21,000
dragging these sort of trailer behind

637
00:34:21,000 --> 00:34:23,000
with these 50 foot lengths of pipe,

638
00:34:23,000 --> 00:34:25,000
and you'd have to throw it all off the back of the tractor

639
00:34:25,000 --> 00:34:27,000
in order, and then you'd have to sort of hook it all up

640
00:34:27,000 --> 00:34:29,000
and you'd spray the crops,

641
00:34:29,000 --> 00:34:30,000
and then you'd pick the thing up

642
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:32,000
and you'd move it over 50 yards and do it all again.

643
00:34:33,000 --> 00:34:35,000
And so because you could never throw it

644
00:34:35,000 --> 00:34:38,000
out of the back of the trailer straight,

645
00:34:38,000 --> 00:34:41,000
these things had a little bit of a give

646
00:34:41,000 --> 00:34:43,000
in the coupling, so it would do like this.

647
00:34:43,000 --> 00:34:45,000
So what the guy did was he suspended it

648
00:34:45,000 --> 00:34:50,000
on these frames that he welded up.

649
00:34:50,000 --> 00:34:52,000
Then this is the same old pipe,

650
00:34:52,000 --> 00:34:55,000
and this is, you know, agricultural implement wheels,

651
00:34:55,000 --> 00:34:57,000
you know, his old farm tractor wheels.

652
00:34:57,000 --> 00:34:59,000
And this is sort of a cylinder.

653
00:34:59,000 --> 00:35:02,000
And what he used to do was to run the water through here

654
00:35:02,000 --> 00:35:04,000
and divert it into these pistons

655
00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:06,000
when he wanted the wheels to turn.

656
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:08,000
And these wires here, basically,

657
00:35:08,000 --> 00:35:10,000
he would set the outermost tower going like this,

658
00:35:10,000 --> 00:35:12,000
it would create a bend in the pipe,

659
00:35:12,000 --> 00:35:14,000
and that would turn the spigot on in the next one,

660
00:35:14,000 --> 00:35:16,000
and the whole thing would sort of go around like this.

661
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:18,000
It was pretty cool.

662
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:22,000
And it was, of course, commercialized.

663
00:35:22,000 --> 00:35:24,000
Here's the commercial version.

664
00:35:24,000 --> 00:35:27,000
You can see it's considerably glit-stopped, right?

665
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:30,000
We're talking seriously fancy here,

666
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:33,000
but it's the same operating principles.

667
00:35:33,000 --> 00:35:35,000
So I could not resist.

668
00:35:35,000 --> 00:35:37,000
I called up the Valmont Company,

669
00:35:37,000 --> 00:35:39,000
which is this company here.

670
00:35:39,000 --> 00:35:44,000
I said, did you get any inputs from users living or dead?

671
00:35:44,000 --> 00:35:47,000
And they said, certainly not, my child.

672
00:35:47,000 --> 00:35:50,000
It's entirely our innovation from start to finish.

673
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:53,000
I was like, gee, that's really interesting.

674
00:35:53,000 --> 00:35:57,000
So it's interesting because,

675
00:35:57,000 --> 00:35:59,000
not only the entertaining value here

676
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:02,000
if there's enough credit to go around because everybody takes it,

677
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:05,000
but you'll see that it's very interesting in the sense

678
00:36:05,000 --> 00:36:09,000
that if companies don't understand where their innovations come from,

679
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:11,000
they can't do it again.

680
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:14,000
And so often when I go to these companies

681
00:36:14,000 --> 00:36:19,000
and I see they're aware of the importance of history

682
00:36:19,000 --> 00:36:21,000
and so they might even write some books

683
00:36:21,000 --> 00:36:24,000
on how we developed our innovations.

684
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:28,000
And the fascinating thing is these histories are usually wrong.

685
00:36:28,000 --> 00:36:30,000
Here's a few more.

686
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:38,000
The World Wide Web, a user innovation.

687
00:36:38,000 --> 00:36:41,000
He wasn't Microsoft.

688
00:36:41,000 --> 00:36:43,000
He wasn't even necessarily a customer of Microsoft.

689
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:48,000
It was something that he wanted.

690
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:52,000
Product innovations by consumers.

691
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:53,000
Personal care.

692
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:56,000
Anybody know about protein-based hair conditioners and stuff like that?

693
00:36:56,000 --> 00:36:58,000
Know about those?

694
00:36:58,000 --> 00:37:02,000
I certainly don't, but anyway, you know about those?

695
00:37:02,000 --> 00:37:06,000
It helps the hair somehow in some magical way.

696
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:09,000
And Procter & Gamble or whoever was very proud

697
00:37:09,000 --> 00:37:12,000
about the idea that they had come up with this.

698
00:37:12,000 --> 00:37:15,000
But before that, what?

699
00:37:15,000 --> 00:37:17,000
We're putting all kinds of stuff in our hair.

700
00:37:17,000 --> 00:37:18,000
Yes.

701
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:19,000
Yeah.

702
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:24,000
Eggs and dead beer and honey.

703
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:25,000
Amazingly discussed.

704
00:37:25,000 --> 00:37:28,000
Avocado, mayonnaise.

705
00:37:28,000 --> 00:37:29,000
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

706
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:33,000
With the same principle being involved.

707
00:37:33,000 --> 00:37:34,000
Right?

708
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:39,000
So in other words, the users had done it for themselves.

709
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:41,000
Sports equipment.

710
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:43,000
Mountain bike.

711
00:37:43,000 --> 00:37:48,000
The mountain bike was not developed by Schwinn or a manufacturer.

712
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:53,000
Does anybody know how the mountain bike was developed?

713
00:37:53,000 --> 00:37:58,000
It was developed by users taking a Schwinn or some other bike and saying,

714
00:37:58,000 --> 00:37:59,000
you know what?

715
00:37:59,000 --> 00:38:00,000
I'm tired.

716
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:01,000
I'm riding down the road.

717
00:38:01,000 --> 00:38:04,000
I think what I'm going to do is ride down a mountain.

718
00:38:04,000 --> 00:38:09,000
So they ride down the mountain on this bike and the bike would fall apart.

719
00:38:09,000 --> 00:38:13,000
And they'd bring it to the dealer and they'd say,

720
00:38:13,000 --> 00:38:16,000
hey, there's a warranty on this, right?

721
00:38:16,000 --> 00:38:21,000
The dealer would say, yeah, looks a little crushed to me.

722
00:38:21,000 --> 00:38:22,000
What did you do with it?

723
00:38:22,000 --> 00:38:24,000
Well, I rode it down a mountain.

724
00:38:24,000 --> 00:38:25,000
Oh, well, warranty void.

725
00:38:25,000 --> 00:38:26,000
You shouldn't ride it down mountains.

726
00:38:26,000 --> 00:38:29,000
It's not for that.

727
00:38:29,000 --> 00:38:34,000
So basically what then happened was that these kids started improving the bikes,

728
00:38:34,000 --> 00:38:36,000
making them better, making them stronger.

729
00:38:36,000 --> 00:38:38,000
Every time they fell apart, they'd fix them up.

730
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:43,000
Eventually you ended up with a mountain bike and manufacturers came in.

731
00:38:43,000 --> 00:38:47,000
So now I want you to think about it.

732
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:50,000
Here's your exercise.

733
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:52,000
Identify your own examples of user innovation.

734
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:57,000
What we want to do here is test this idea out in your own minds.

735
00:38:57,000 --> 00:39:01,000
So what I'm proposing is for five minutes, you take a look,

736
00:39:01,000 --> 00:39:04,000
you talk to your neighbor about this.

737
00:39:04,000 --> 00:39:10,000
Think of your experience as a firm or thinking of your experience as a consumer.

738
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:15,000
In the case of a firm, did a product modification by a customer ever come to your attention?

739
00:39:15,000 --> 00:39:17,000
And by the way, what did you do about it?

740
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:22,000
Or did your firm, acting as a customer, basically modify a product sold to you

741
00:39:22,000 --> 00:39:24,000
or develop something for themselves?

742
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:27,000
And as a user, if you don't think about those,

743
00:39:27,000 --> 00:39:30,000
do you really care about something like a sport or type of cooking?

744
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:34,000
Do you really modify a commercial product you bought?

745
00:39:34,000 --> 00:39:37,000
So say hello to your neighbor, good plan,

746
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:41,000
and tell them about an innovation you did or know about that's a user innovation.

747
00:39:41,000 --> 00:39:44,000
Test this idea out.

748
00:39:44,000 --> 00:39:50,000
Okay, let's see.

749
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:53,000
Who's got something wonderful?

750
00:39:53,000 --> 00:39:58,000
Usually what happens at this point is that you volunteer the person next to you.

751
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:00,000
They want to share, as we say. Yes, please.

752
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:05,000
I'll share a user innovation that I've witnessed coming up as a child.

753
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:07,000
And that is, I went to Brooklyn, Philadelphia,

754
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:13,000
and we used to listen to a lot of records on turntables.

755
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:17,000
And at that time, a lot of people around my neighbor figured out,

756
00:40:17,000 --> 00:40:21,000
as a kid, that if you took the record and you manipulated it,

757
00:40:21,000 --> 00:40:23,000
you get these scratching sounds.

758
00:40:23,000 --> 00:40:27,000
So after a while, but the problem was the needle was,

759
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:30,000
turntable wasn't built to do that, so the needle would skip

760
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:34,000
because there wasn't enough pressure to keep it steady.

761
00:40:34,000 --> 00:40:39,000
So we would take a penny and usually take some tape and put,

762
00:40:39,000 --> 00:40:42,000
or even gum at a particular part, put it underneath of it,

763
00:40:42,000 --> 00:40:45,000
and it would actually help the needle stay still.

764
00:40:45,000 --> 00:40:48,000
